  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What are the three things a fire needs?",
      "a": "Fuel, heat and oxygen. Fuel is anything that can burn, oxygen comes from the air, and heat gets the reaction started. Remove any one and the fire goes out.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the fire triangle?",
        "What makes fire burn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does water put out a fire?",
      "a": "Water soaks up a lot of heat, especially as it turns into steam, so it cools the fuel below the temperature it needs to burn. The steam also pushes some air away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does water stop fire?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why should you never throw water on an oil fire?",
      "a": "The water sinks under the very hot oil and instantly flashes into steam, which blasts burning oil into the air as a fireball. Turn off the heat and cover the pan with a lid or fire blanket instead.",
      "asks": [
        "What do you do if a chip pan catches fire?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a fire blanket put out a fire?",
      "a": "It covers the flames and stops fresh air reaching them. Without oxygen the burning stops.",
      "asks": [
        "How does smothering a fire work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does blowing on a campfire make it bigger?",
      "a": "A gentle breath pushes in extra oxygen, so the hot fuel burns faster. A small candle flame is different: a puff cools it and blows its fuel away.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do bellows help a fire?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Air is about 21 percent oxygen, and most flames go out when the oxygen level falls below roughly 15 percent.",
    "Paper catches fire by itself at around 230 degrees Celsius."
  ],
